The MAS Guidelines on AI Risk Management bind from 7 October 2027. We built an open-source toolkit that turns them into machine-checkable controls, then measured how far that actually works.

In the box:
- A machine-readable catalogue: {{catalogue.paragraphs}} paragraphs, {{catalogue.expectations}} expectations, {{catalogue.normative_footnotes}} footnotes that carry requirements (the fn25 agent attributes among them).
- {{controls.total}} controls, each citing the paragraph it serves and labelled by how far it can be automated.
- A {{rq4.mapping_rows}}-row crosswalk to ISO/IEC 42001, NIST AI RMF and its GenAI Profile, the EU AI Act, FEAT and the 2024 MAS Information Paper.
- A worked example on a fictional bank with {{portfolio.use_cases}} synthetic use cases, and five pre-registered experiments.

The headline: {{rq1.auto_share}} of expectations admit a fully automated check. Section 4 (inventory, materiality) is {{rq1.section4_auto_share}} automatable; Section 3 (governance) is {{rq1.section3_auto_share}}.

Of {{verdicts.total}} hypotheses fixed before the experiments ran, {{verdicts.refuted}} were refuted. All of them are in the paper, with the thresholds they failed.

Read it: https://thanatchon36.github.io/airmkit/paper/

Not affiliated with MAS. Not legal advice. Apache-2.0.

